From 7ae01c7e5461158daabc271af5849dca0dc4dde8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Enrico Bartky <enrico.bartky@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 13:08:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Unbox the other side of a mixed numeric comparison, item 3-5

Item 4-5's probe produced this by accident: the control loop every measurement in
the roadmap treats as a floor was itself paying a box per iteration. The same
loop with a literal bound ran at 8.36 ns and 0 bytes against 33.77 ns and 32.

The cause is not the parameter, and that changes the fix. Item 3-3 recorded the
gap as "a parameter cannot reach the numeric tier", which is true and is not what
costs the box: `i` IS a raw double, `n` is a JSValue, and `<` had a native form
only when BOTH operands were already doubles - so the mixed case fell through to
the generic operator and boxed the raw side to meet it. Unboxing the other side
instead needs no entry guard and no second body, and covers strictly more:
`for (var i = 0; i < a.length; i++)` is a property read, not a parameter, and was
boxed for the same reason.

Sound because relational comparison runs ToPrimitive on both operands and
ToPrimitive of a Number is that Number - no valueOf, no toString, no observable
effect - so the guarded path is the same path with the same answer and everything
else reaches the operator it reached before. Only `<` and `>`, for the reason the
neighbouring code already records: the backend emits an ORDERED compare for
`<=`/`>=`, which answers true on NaN where JavaScript answers false.

Block-declared locals rather than pooled temporaries, and that is correctness
rather than style. Both operands are spilled, and the temporaries are needed
after the operands have already been compiled - so a pooled temp could be one a
sub-expression released while being built, and the second spill would clobber the
first operand. `i < obj.m()` is enough to reach it.

MixedNumericComparisonTests, 33 cases, all about semantics: every relation in
both directions and with the native side on either side; NaN on each side; +/-0;
+/-Infinity; strings, null, undefined, booleans, arrays, objects, a Number
wrapper, a BigInt, a Symbol that must still throw; valueOf called exactly once
per comparison and only on the fallback; source-order evaluation once each; a
throwing valueOf; a non-numeric loop bound; and a bound whose type changes
mid-loop. All 33 pass on the unmodified compiler too, so they pin the existing
semantics rather than describe the change.

On its shape it is 3.4x: 33.77 -> 10.03 ns and 32 -> 0 bytes an iteration.

On the corpus it is invisible - 0.997x bytes, 0.995x time over four paired Octane
rounds - and why is the finding. The emission is not the problem: 390 relational
comparisons take the new form, 59% of those that could. What is on the other side
is. Only 203 of 4 029 scalar locals in the seven suites reach the numeric tier,
5.0%. That is the ceiling on 3-0, 3-3 and 3-5 alike - each correct, each large on
its shape, each meeting the same CanScalarReplaceLocals gate, which requires no
nested functions, no captured names, no eval and no with. It is the same gate
that bounds phase 4's tiering candidates, found from the other side in item 4-2.

Widening it becomes new item 3-6, and it is worth more than any local-
representation item already landed. It also answers what 3-4 was told to wait
for: the gap largely survives unboxed locals, because the unboxing reaches 5% of
them.

Adds compile-time counters for both comparison forms and reports them, plus
per-suite allocation, from --specializing-tier. Repository suite: 7 872 tests
across 13 projects, 0 failures.
